We investigate non-stochastic bandits and experts in a delayed context, where delays are contingent upon both the time and arm choice. While delayed settings for stochastic bandits and experts have been well-examined, considering non-stochastic scenarios presents additional complexities. In the case of experts, we develop a policy ensuring a $\frac{K}{B}\log_T$ regret, given a time horizon of $T$; this policy is optimal, as confirmed by a lower bound. Furthermore, we demonstrate that when $T$ is independent of $K$, a bandit problem under identical conditions is problem-specific and has a minimum regret of $\Omega(\sqrt{K})$, in addition to proving that any method with a regret of $O(\sqrt{K})$ is optimal. However, when $T$ is in relation to $K$, we demonstrate a regret of \begin{align*}
&\Omega\left(\sqrt{K}+\frac{K}{\sqrt{T}}+\frac{T\sqrt{K}}{B}\right) \\
&\text{for }T=O\left(2^2\cdot\sqrt{K}\cdot\log_2(K/B)\right).
\end{align*} Additionally, we show a regret of \begin{align*}
